# Patent Claim: Media Airing Attribution System

**Claim 1.** A computer system for attributing web orders to media airings, comprising:

one or more processors; and

a non-transitory computer-readable memory storing instructions that, when executed by the one or more processors, cause the system to:

receive media data comprising a plurality of media airing records, each media airing record specifying at least an airing timestamp and a media identifier associated with a broadcast media event;

receive web tracking transaction data comprising a plurality of web order records, each web order record specifying at least a transaction timestamp and one or more attributes associated with a web order originating from a client device;

establish a baseline web activity level representing an expected volume of web orders during a time period absent influence from a media airing;

for a given media airing record, define an attribution time window relative to the airing timestamp of the given media airing record;

identify, from the plurality of web order records, a subset of web order records having transaction timestamps falling within the attribution time window;

calculate a lift value representing a quantity of web orders in the subset that exceeds the baseline web activity level within the attribution time window;

attribute one or more of the web orders in the subset to the given media airing record based at least in part on the calculated lift value; and

generate an attribution output associating the attributed one or more web orders with the media identifier of the given media airing record.

---

**Dependent claims** you might add:

**Claim 2.** The system of claim 1, wherein the attribution time window comprises a decay function that weights web order records inversely proportional to a temporal distance between the transaction timestamp and the airing timestamp.

**Claim 3.** The system of claim 1, wherein the instructions further cause the system to resolve overlapping attribution windows from multiple media airing records by allocating individual web orders among competing media airings according to a probabilistic allocation model.

**Claim 4.** The system of claim 1, wherein the baseline web activity level is determined using historical web tracking transaction data collected during time periods lacking associated media airings.

**Claim 5.** The system of claim 1, wherein each media airing record further comprises a media attribute selected from a network identifier, a geographic market, a daypart, a program identifier, and a creative identifier, and wherein the attribution output aggregates attributed web orders by the media attribute.

---

